1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:04,340
The last week before the SAT isn't
the time to learn everything.

2
00:00:04,360 --> 00:00:07,300
It's the time to sharpen
what you already know,

3
00:00:07,320 --> 00:00:12,260
fix your most common mistakes, and make
sure nothing goes wrong on test morning.

4
00:00:12,280 --> 00:00:16,930
Here's a plan, day by day, from one
week out to the moment you walk in.

5
00:00:17,386 --> 00:00:21,046
The most common mistake
this week is panic studying.

6
00:00:21,066 --> 00:00:24,006
Students cram new topics, stay up late,

7
00:00:24,026 --> 00:00:25,595
and show up tired.

8
00:00:25,625 --> 00:00:29,635
That trades points you already have
for points you probably won't get.

9
00:00:29,746 --> 00:00:32,046
So the rule for this week is simple.

10
00:00:32,066 --> 00:00:33,086
Review.

11
00:00:33,106 --> 00:00:34,276
Don't cram.

12
00:00:34,676 --> 00:00:35,456
Day seven.

13
00:00:35,476 --> 00:00:36,216
One week out.

14
00:00:36,236 --> 00:00:39,136
Take a full-length
practice test in Bluebook,

15
00:00:39,156 --> 00:00:41,686
the same app you'll use on test day.

16
00:00:41,996 --> 00:00:45,376
The practice tests there are
timed like the real thing,

17
00:00:45,396 --> 00:00:50,295
and College Board recommends taking at
least one with the timer on and breaks

18
00:00:50,315 --> 00:00:51,566
like test day.

19
00:00:52,013 --> 00:00:53,473
Make it feel like test day.

20
00:00:53,493 --> 00:00:54,513
Start in the morning.

21
00:00:54,533 --> 00:00:56,793
Sit at a desk, not on your bed.

22
00:00:56,813 --> 00:00:58,793
Put your phone in another room.

23
00:00:58,813 --> 00:01:00,753
Use real scratch paper.

24
00:01:00,773 --> 00:01:06,342
And take only the ten-minute break
between Reading and Writing and Math.

25
00:01:06,828 --> 00:01:10,127
Don't stop to look things up,
and don't pause the clock.

26
00:01:10,147 --> 00:01:12,048
The point isn't just the score.

27
00:01:12,068 --> 00:01:15,568
It's to find out what goes
wrong under real timing,

28
00:01:15,588 --> 00:01:17,728
so the rest of the week can fix it.

29
00:01:17,748 --> 00:01:21,998
And starting today, wake up at the
time you'll wake up on test day.

30
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:23,720
Day six.

31
00:01:23,740 --> 00:01:25,390
Review the test.

32
00:01:25,819 --> 00:01:29,880
After a Bluebook practice test,
your scores show up in My Practice,

33
00:01:29,900 --> 00:01:33,630
where you can review each
question with its explanation.

34
00:01:33,780 --> 00:01:35,719
Go through every question you missed,

35
00:01:35,739 --> 00:01:40,070
and every question you guessed
on, even if you got it right.

36
00:01:40,539 --> 00:01:44,349
For each one, don't just read
the explanation and move on.

37
00:01:44,579 --> 00:01:46,749
Write down why you missed it.

38
00:01:46,939 --> 00:01:50,108
Almost every miss fits one of four labels.

39
00:01:50,555 --> 00:01:52,095
One, content.

40
00:01:52,115 --> 00:01:54,334
You didn't know the rule or the formula.

41
00:01:54,354 --> 00:01:55,895
Two, careless.

42
00:01:55,915 --> 00:02:00,014
You knew it, but misread the
question or made an arithmetic slip.

43
00:02:00,034 --> 00:02:01,694
Three, time.

44
00:02:01,714 --> 00:02:03,854
You rushed it or never got to it.

45
00:02:03,874 --> 00:02:05,415
Four, trap.

46
00:02:05,435 --> 00:02:09,565
You picked an answer that looked right
but didn't answer what was asked.

47
00:02:10,045 --> 00:02:10,905
Quick practice.

48
00:02:10,925 --> 00:02:12,344
Here are three misses.

49
00:02:12,364 --> 00:02:13,855
Label each one.

50
00:02:14,125 --> 00:02:18,334
First, you solved for x, but the
question asked for x plus two.

51
00:02:18,485 --> 00:02:22,774
Second, you knew the comma rule,
but ran out of time and guessed.

52
00:02:23,124 --> 00:02:27,975
Third, you'd never learned how to get
a circle's radius from its equation.

53
00:02:28,446 --> 00:02:29,816
Pause and try it.

54
00:02:35,582 --> 00:02:37,672
The first is a trap.

55
00:02:38,222 --> 00:02:41,512
You did the math right and
answered the wrong question.

56
00:02:41,982 --> 00:02:44,352
The second is time.

57
00:02:44,502 --> 00:02:46,592
The third is content.

58
00:02:46,821 --> 00:02:49,391
And each one has a different fix.

59
00:02:49,821 --> 00:02:53,992
Drilling circle problems wouldn't
help the first two at all.

60
00:02:54,415 --> 00:02:56,145
Now count the labels.

61
00:02:56,374 --> 00:02:58,675
Content misses get drills.

62
00:02:58,695 --> 00:03:03,355
Careless misses get a habit, like
rereading what the question asks for before

63
00:03:03,375 --> 00:03:04,275
you answer.

64
00:03:04,295 --> 00:03:06,714
Time misses get pacing practice.

65
00:03:06,734 --> 00:03:09,755
Trap misses mean slowing
down on the last step.

66
00:03:09,775 --> 00:03:12,105
Your counts decide the rest of the week.

67
00:03:12,615 --> 00:03:15,155
Days five and four are for drills.

68
00:03:15,175 --> 00:03:18,435
Pick the two skills where
you lost the most points.

69
00:03:18,455 --> 00:03:23,115
Then open the College Board's
free SAT Suite Question Bank.

70
00:03:23,135 --> 00:03:26,035
You can filter it by section, domain,

71
00:03:26,055 --> 00:03:28,234
skill, and difficulty.

72
00:03:28,254 --> 00:03:32,675
Start at medium, and move up to hard
once most of the medium ones are going

73
00:03:32,695 --> 00:03:33,695
right.

74
00:03:33,744 --> 00:03:37,043
Do about fifteen to twenty
questions per skill,

75
00:03:37,063 --> 00:03:37,924
per day.

76
00:03:37,944 --> 00:03:40,644
Start untimed, so you
get the method right.

77
00:03:40,664 --> 00:03:43,324
Then do a second set on a clock.

78
00:03:43,344 --> 00:03:47,564
That's about seventy-one seconds a
question for Reading and Writing,

79
00:03:47,584 --> 00:03:50,354
and about ninety-five for math.

80
00:03:50,799 --> 00:03:54,089
When you miss one, add it
to your log with its label.

81
00:03:54,319 --> 00:03:57,979
By day four, you should see
fewer of the same mistake.

82
00:03:57,999 --> 00:04:00,778
If you don't, that's the
skill to keep drilling.

83
00:04:00,798 --> 00:04:02,608
Don't switch to something new.

84
00:04:03,065 --> 00:04:04,795
One more job in this window.

85
00:04:04,825 --> 00:04:09,645
Exam setup opens in Bluebook one
to five days before your test,

86
00:04:09,665 --> 00:04:11,845
and you have to finish
it by the day before.

87
00:04:11,865 --> 00:04:15,165
Do it early, on the exact
device you're bringing.

88
00:04:15,185 --> 00:04:17,794
At the end, you get your admission ticket.

89
00:04:18,231 --> 00:04:20,970
Print the ticket, or email it to yourself.

90
00:04:20,990 --> 00:04:24,081
College Board says a
printed ticket is preferred.

91
00:04:24,151 --> 00:04:25,491
Then read it.

92
00:04:25,511 --> 00:04:28,251
It has your test center
and your arrival time,

93
00:04:28,271 --> 00:04:31,361
and sometimes special
instructions about your center.

94
00:04:31,776 --> 00:04:32,716
Day three.

95
00:04:32,736 --> 00:04:36,556
Keep drilling your weakest
skill, and add a Desmos warm-up.

96
00:04:36,576 --> 00:04:40,755
Bluebook has the Desmos
graphing calculator built in.

97
00:04:40,775 --> 00:04:43,075
The goal today is speed.

98
00:04:43,095 --> 00:04:47,585
You want the basic moves to
be automatic before test day.

99
00:04:48,026 --> 00:04:48,646
Try this one.

100
00:04:48,666 --> 00:04:54,406
What is the positive solution to the
equation three x squared minus five x minus

101
00:04:54,426 --> 00:04:56,116
two equals zero?

102
00:04:56,529 --> 00:04:57,899
Pause and try it.

103
00:05:03,666 --> 00:05:09,286
In Desmos, type y equals three x
squared minus five x minus two.

104
00:05:09,306 --> 00:05:13,965
Click the curve, and the points
where it crosses the x axis appear.

105
00:05:13,985 --> 00:05:19,045
One is negative one third, which Desmos
shows as negative zero point three three

106
00:05:19,065 --> 00:05:19,766
three.

107
00:05:19,786 --> 00:05:21,126
The other is two.

108
00:05:21,146 --> 00:05:23,436
So the positive solution is two.

109
00:05:23,850 --> 00:05:27,419
By hand, it factors into three x plus one,

110
00:05:27,450 --> 00:05:29,550
times x minus two.

111
00:05:29,570 --> 00:05:30,470
Same answer.

112
00:05:30,490 --> 00:05:33,030
Use whichever way is faster for you.

113
00:05:33,050 --> 00:05:36,579
And practice with the calculator
you'll use on test day.

114
00:05:36,730 --> 00:05:39,830
Bluebook's test preview
lets you try every tool,

115
00:05:39,850 --> 00:05:41,020
untimed.

116
00:05:41,448 --> 00:05:43,588
Also on day three, check your ID.

117
00:05:43,608 --> 00:05:48,588
It has to be a physical photo
ID, not a picture on your phone.

118
00:05:48,608 --> 00:05:49,987
It can't be expired.

119
00:05:50,007 --> 00:05:53,628
And the name has to match
your admission ticket exactly.

120
00:05:53,648 --> 00:05:56,388
If something's wrong,
you want to find out now,

121
00:05:56,408 --> 00:05:57,538
not at the door.

122
00:05:58,053 --> 00:05:58,953
Day two.

123
00:05:58,973 --> 00:06:00,183
Go lighter.

124
00:06:00,213 --> 00:06:03,033
Reread your mistake log, top to bottom.

125
00:06:03,053 --> 00:06:05,873
That log is the best study guide you have,

126
00:06:05,893 --> 00:06:08,223
because it's built from your own mistakes.

127
00:06:08,753 --> 00:06:11,773
Do a short mixed set,
maybe twenty minutes,

128
00:06:11,793 --> 00:06:13,243
just to stay sharp.

129
00:06:13,593 --> 00:06:17,653
Then look over the math reference
sheet so you know what's on it,

130
00:06:17,673 --> 00:06:19,002
and what isn't.

131
00:06:19,113 --> 00:06:20,892
Don't start any new topic.

132
00:06:20,912 --> 00:06:24,162
If it's brand new to you now,
it isn't worth the stress.

133
00:06:24,703 --> 00:06:25,923
Then logistics.

134
00:06:25,943 --> 00:06:29,203
Look up your test center and
how long it takes to get there.

135
00:06:29,223 --> 00:06:33,443
In the days before the test, College
Board says to keep checking that your test

136
00:06:33,463 --> 00:06:35,883
center hasn't changed or closed.

137
00:06:35,903 --> 00:06:38,483
Check your test card on
the Bluebook homepage,

138
00:06:38,503 --> 00:06:42,713
your email, and College Board's
test center closings page.

139
00:06:43,170 --> 00:06:47,110
Before the night before, here's
what not to do this week.

140
00:06:47,130 --> 00:06:49,030
Don't cram new content.

141
00:06:49,050 --> 00:06:52,469
And don't take another full
practice test in the last two days.

142
00:06:52,489 --> 00:06:57,739
It's tiring, and one off day can
shake your confidence for no reason.

143
00:06:58,226 --> 00:06:59,406
Don't change your sleep.

144
00:06:59,426 --> 00:07:02,566
Keep the same bedtime and
wake-up time all week.

145
00:07:02,586 --> 00:07:05,166
A late night, or a big
shift the night before,

146
00:07:05,186 --> 00:07:06,765
just leaves you tired.

147
00:07:06,785 --> 00:07:08,406
And don't switch calculators.

148
00:07:08,426 --> 00:07:12,126
If you bring your own, College
Board says to bring one you know.

149
00:07:12,146 --> 00:07:15,636
Otherwise, use the built-in
Desmos you've practiced with.

150
00:07:16,176 --> 00:07:18,106
Day one, the night before.

151
00:07:18,696 --> 00:07:22,156
First, if exam setup
isn't done, do it now.

152
00:07:22,176 --> 00:07:23,626
Today is the deadline.

153
00:07:23,696 --> 00:07:29,235
Then open Bluebook on your device, so you
know it starts up and is on the latest

154
00:07:29,255 --> 00:07:30,255
version.

155
00:07:30,580 --> 00:07:31,920
Now pack your bag.

156
00:07:31,940 --> 00:07:34,430
Your testing device, fully charged.

157
00:07:34,860 --> 00:07:38,829
College Board says it will need to
stay on for three to four hours.

158
00:07:38,899 --> 00:07:41,710
Your admission ticket, printed if you can.

159
00:07:41,860 --> 00:07:43,750
Your physical photo ID.

160
00:07:43,860 --> 00:07:46,480
Pencils or pens for scratch work.

161
00:07:46,500 --> 00:07:49,190
Your College Board username and password.

162
00:07:49,230 --> 00:07:51,960
And your calculator, if
you're bringing your own.

163
00:07:52,405 --> 00:07:55,585
Good to add: a charger
or portable battery,

164
00:07:55,605 --> 00:07:58,545
though College Board can't
promise you an outlet.

165
00:07:58,565 --> 00:08:03,425
A watch with no audible alarm, a
drink and a snack for the break,

166
00:08:03,445 --> 00:08:05,624
and a backup device if you have one.

167
00:08:05,644 --> 00:08:09,425
Staff will collect your phone or have
you store it away from your desk,

168
00:08:09,445 --> 00:08:11,295
so don't count on it for anything.

169
00:08:11,746 --> 00:08:13,926
Study for thirty minutes at most.

170
00:08:13,946 --> 00:08:16,206
Reread your log, then stop.

171
00:08:16,226 --> 00:08:18,566
Lay out your clothes, set two alarms,

172
00:08:18,586 --> 00:08:20,606
and go to bed at your normal time.

173
00:08:20,626 --> 00:08:21,606
You've done the work.

174
00:08:21,626 --> 00:08:24,235
Tonight is about showing up rested.

175
00:08:24,692 --> 00:08:25,952
Test morning.

176
00:08:25,972 --> 00:08:28,821
Eat a normal breakfast,
the kind you usually eat.

177
00:08:28,892 --> 00:08:30,992
Check your bag one more time: ticket,

178
00:08:31,012 --> 00:08:32,952
ID, charged device.

179
00:08:32,972 --> 00:08:35,622
Then close and reopen Bluebook.

180
00:08:35,972 --> 00:08:39,592
College Board says to do that on test
day morning to check for last-minute

181
00:08:39,612 --> 00:08:42,782
messages and updated
test center information.

182
00:08:43,206 --> 00:08:46,016
Check your admission ticket
for your arrival time.

183
00:08:46,246 --> 00:08:49,346
Test centers open at 7:45 in the morning,

184
00:08:49,366 --> 00:08:53,505
and doors close at eight, unless
your ticket says otherwise.

185
00:08:53,525 --> 00:08:56,546
If you're borrowing a
device from College Board,

186
00:08:56,566 --> 00:08:59,376
arrive earlier, at 7:15.

187
00:08:59,858 --> 00:09:01,678
On the way, don't study.

188
00:09:01,698 --> 00:09:06,478
If you want a warm-up, do two or three
easy questions you've already solved,

189
00:09:06,498 --> 00:09:08,237
just to get your brain moving.

190
00:09:08,257 --> 00:09:10,468
Nothing new, and nothing hard.

191
00:09:10,902 --> 00:09:12,482
So here's the week.

192
00:09:12,502 --> 00:09:14,962
Day seven, a full timed test.

193
00:09:14,982 --> 00:09:17,592
Day six, label every miss.

194
00:09:17,702 --> 00:09:20,722
Days five and four, drill
your top two skills,

195
00:09:20,742 --> 00:09:22,722
and finish exam setup.

196
00:09:22,742 --> 00:09:25,832
Day three, Desmos and your ID.

197
00:09:25,901 --> 00:09:28,762
Day two, light review and logistics.

198
00:09:28,782 --> 00:09:31,042
Day one, pack and sleep.

199
00:09:31,062 --> 00:09:34,272
Then test morning, on time and calm.

200
00:09:34,709 --> 00:09:38,519
If your test isn't this week,
save this video for when it is.

201
00:09:38,709 --> 00:09:45,679
Next in this series: every SAT math
formula that's not on the reference sheet.

202
00:09:45,829 --> 00:09:47,958
If this helped, subscribe.

203
00:09:48,108 --> 00:09:49,198
And good luck.
